<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-problems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Problems</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The problems collected here are grouped by topic rather than by the week in which
      they were assigned, so that each group sits alongside the material of the chapter it
      belongs to. Problems on topics not developed in these notes have been omitted.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-problems-linear-systems">
    <title>Systems of Linear Equations</title>
    <exercises>


    <exercise xml:id="lab-geometry-of-solutions">
      <title>The Geometry of a Solution Set</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          You have a system of <m>k</m> equations in two variables, <m>k \geq 2</m>.
          Explain the geometric significance of
          <ol>
            <li><p>no solution;</p></li>
            <li><p>a unique solution;</p></li>
            <li><p>an infinite number of solutions.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Each equation is a line in the plane, and a solution is a point lying on
          <em>every</em> one of them, as in
          <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(1) No solution.</term> The <m>k</m> lines have no point in common. This
          happens as soon as two of them are parallel and distinct, but it can also happen
          with no two parallel <mdash /> three lines meeting pairwise in three different
          points already have no common point.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2) A unique solution.</term> All <m>k</m> lines pass through the same
          single point.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3) Infinitely many solutions.</term> All <m>k</m> equations describe the
          same line, so every point of that line is a solution.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="lab-shape-consistent-unique">
      <title>Reading Consistency off an Echelon Shape</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In each augmented matrix below, <m>*</m> denotes an arbitrary number and
          <m>\blacksquare</m> a nonzero number. Determine whether the corresponding system
          is consistent, and if so whether the solution is unique.
          <md>
            \text{(a)} \quad
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare \amp *
            \end{array} \right]
          </md>
          <md>
            \text{(b)} \quad
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccccc|c}
              \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare \amp *
            \end{array} \right]
          </md>
          <md>
            \text{(c)} \quad
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccccc|c}
              \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp * \amp \blacksquare
            \end{array} \right]
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> Every row has a leading entry, so no row reads
          <m>0 = d</m> with <m>d \neq 0</m> and the system is consistent by
          <xref ref="rem-inconsistency-test" text="type-global" />. Every column of the
          coefficient matrix also has a leading entry, so there are no free variables and
          the solution is <term>unique</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> Again every row has a leading entry, so the system is
          consistent. But column <m>4</m> has no leading entry, so <m>x_4</m> is a free
          variable and there are <term>infinitely many solutions</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> This one needs a further step. Using the leading entry in row
          <m>3</m> to clear the entry below it makes the <m>(4,5)</m> entry zero, while
          the last column entry stays nonzero, because the <m>(3,6)</m> entry is
          <m>0</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccccc|c}
              \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
              0 \amp \blacksquare \amp * \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \blacksquare
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          The last row now reads
          <m>0x_1 + 0x_2 + 0x_3 + 0x_4 + 0x_5 = \blacksquare</m>, that is
          <m>0 = \blacksquare \neq 0</m>. The system is <term>inconsistent</term>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="lab-h-and-k">
      <title>Choosing Parameters to Control the Outcome</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Choose <m>h</m> and <m>k</m> so that the system with augmented matrix
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp h \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp k \end{array} \right]
          </md>
          has (a) one solution, (b) no solution, (c) infinitely many solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          One row operation puts the matrix in echelon form:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp h \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp k \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp h \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 4 - 2h \amp k - 4 \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          Everything depends on whether <m>4 - 2h</m> is zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(a) One solution.</term> If <m>4 - 2h \neq 0</m>, i.e.
          <m>h \neq 2</m>, then both columns have a leading entry, there are no free
          variables, and the solution is unique <mdash /> whatever <m>k</m> is.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b) No solution.</term> If <m>h = 2</m> and <m>k \neq 4</m>, the second
          row reads <m>0 = k - 4 \neq 0</m>, so the system is inconsistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c) Infinitely many solutions.</term> If <m>h = 2</m> and <m>k = 4</m>,
          the second row is entirely zero, <m>x_2</m> is free, and there are infinitely
          many solutions.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="lab-general-solution-2x3">
      <title>A General Solution in Parametric Form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution of the system whose augmented matrix is
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 2 \end{array} \right]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 2 \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 1 \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp -1 \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp -1 \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are pivot columns, so <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are
          basic and <m>z</m> is free. Setting <m>z = s</m>, the rows read
          <m>x + 4s = 2</m> and <m>y - 4s = -1</m>, so the solution set in parametric form
          is
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -4s + 2 \\ 4s - 1 \\ s \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad s \in \R.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="lab-complete-solution-3x3">
      <title>The Complete Solution of a System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give the complete solution to the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              3x - y + 4z \amp = 6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y + 8z \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -2x + y \phantom{{}+ 8z} \amp = -4
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing the augmented matrix,
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              3 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 8 \amp 0 \\
              -2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -4
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 8 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The third row is zero, so the system is consistent and <m>z</m> is a free
          variable. With <m>z = t</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = 2 - 4t
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -8t
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = t, \qquad t \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-problems-matrix-operations">
    <title>Matrix Operations</title>
    <exercises>


    <exercise xml:id="lab-which-products-exist">
      <title>Which Combinations Are Defined?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 7 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\ -3 \amp 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            C = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            E = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Find, if possible, <m>-3A</m>, <m>3B - A</m>, <m>AC</m>, <m>CB</m>,
          <m>AE</m>, and <m>EA</m>. If a combination is not possible, explain why.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>Scalar multiple and sum.</term> Both are computed entry by entry, and
          <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same size, so both are defined:
          <md>
            -3A = \begin{bmatrix} -3 \amp -6 \amp -9 \\ -6 \amp -3 \amp -21 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            3B - A = \begin{bmatrix} 8 \amp -5 \amp 3 \\ -11 \amp 5 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term><m>AC</m> is not possible.</term> <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 3</m> and
          <m>C</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m>. By
          <xref ref="rem-product-size" text="type-global" /> the product needs the number
          of columns of <m>A</m> to equal the number of rows of <m>C</m>, and
          <m>3 \neq 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term><m>CB</m> is defined</term> and is <m>2 \times 3</m>:
          <md>
            CB = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\ -3 \amp 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
               = \begin{bmatrix} -3 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\ 6 \amp -1 \amp 7 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term><m>AE</m> and <m>EA</m> are not possible.</term> <m>A</m> is
          <m>2 \times 3</m> and <m>E</m> is <m>2 \times 1</m>: for <m>AE</m> we would need
          <m>3 = 2</m>, and for <m>EA</m> we would need <m>1 = 2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="lab-products-both-orders">
      <title>Products in Both Orders</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp -2 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            C = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ -3 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Find, if possible, <m>AB</m>, <m>BA</m>, <m>AC</m>, <m>CA</m>, <m>BC</m>, and
          <m>CB</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The sizes are <m>A_{3 \times 2}</m>, <m>B_{2 \times 3}</m>,
          <m>C_{3 \times 3}</m>. Checking inner dimensions:
          <md>
            AB = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \amp -4 \\ -4 \amp 1 \amp 6 \\ 5 \amp 1 \amp -6 \end{bmatrix}_{3 \times 3},
            \qquad
            BA = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \\ -2 \amp -3 \end{bmatrix}_{2 \times 2},
          </md>
          <md>
            CA = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \amp -6 \\ -5 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}_{3 \times 2},
            \qquad
            BC = \begin{bmatrix} 8 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\ 7 \amp 6 \amp -6 \end{bmatrix}_{2 \times 3}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>AC</m> is not possible, since <m>A</m> has <m>2</m> columns while <m>C</m>
          has <m>3</m> rows; and <m>CB</m> is not possible, since <m>C</m> has <m>3</m>
          columns while <m>B</m> has <m>2</m> rows.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that <m>AB</m> and <m>BA</m> are both defined here but are not even the
          same size, a stronger failure of commutativity than
          <xref ref="eg-ab-not-ba" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-problems-linear-combinations">
    <title>Linear Combinations</title>
    <exercises>


    <exercise xml:id="lab-linear-combination">
      <title>Writing One Vector in Terms of Others</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider
          <md>
            \vec{C}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
            \vec{C}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
            \vec{C}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
            \vec{B} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Determine whether <m>\vec{B}</m> can be written as a linear combination of
          <m>\vec{C}_1</m>, <m>\vec{C}_2</m>, <m>\vec{C}_3</m>, and if so give a specific
          such combination.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The question asks whether there exist <m>x, y, z</m> with
          <m>x\vec{C}_1 + y\vec{C}_2 + z\vec{C}_3 = \vec{B}</m>, which by
          <xref ref="def-matrix-vector-product" text="type-global" /> is the system with
          augmented matrix
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
              2 \amp -4 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The system is consistent, so the answer is yes. Here <m>y</m> is free; with
          <m>y = s</m> we get <m>z = 3</m> and <m>x = -1 + 2s</m>, so
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -1 + 2s \\ s \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad s \in \R.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Taking <m>s = 0</m> gives the specific combination
          <m>(x,y,z) = (-1, 0, 3)</m>, and checking it,
          <md>
            -\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            + 0 \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \vec{B}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          That <m>y</m> is free is no accident: <m>\vec{C}_2 = -2\vec{C}_1</m>, so
          <m>\{\vec{C}_1, \vec{C}_2, \vec{C}_3\}</m> is linearly dependent and the
          combination is far from unique.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
</chapter>
